<Project>
  <!--
    Repo-wide MSBuild defaults. Imported automatically by every project
    under this directory before the project's own .fsproj is evaluated.

    NU1510 suppression removed 2026-05-08 in Phase 11.C.1 (Paket -> CPM
    migration). Pre-migration, Paket emitted every transitive package as
    an explicit PackageReference in obj/*.paket.props, which triggered
    NU1510 against framework-provided packages. Post-migration, only
    direct PackageReference items remain (versions centralised in
    Directory.Packages.props), so NU1510 is now a meaningful signal —
    if it ever fires, it points at an explicit reference that should be
    pruned, not noise from generated files.
  -->

  <!--
    Phase 11.C.2 (2026-05-08) — common NuGet pack metadata. SDK companion
    fsprojs inherit these defaults; per-package overrides (PackageId,
    Description, etc.) live in each fsproj's <PropertyGroup>. Non-public-
    surface projects (App composition roots, modules, test harnesses,
    test packs, commercial-internal) opt out with <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    in their own fsproj.

    Initial version 0.1.0 across the board. SemVer-on-0.x policy: minor
    bumps may include breaking changes during 0.x; patch bumps remain
    non-breaking. Per-package <Version> overrides where a package needs
    to advance independently.
  -->
  <!--
    Phase 624 (2026-07-31) — FS0025 (incomplete pattern match) is an
    ERROR, not a hint.

    WHY. FS0025 is non-fatal and names only ONE example of the unmatched
    case. Measured directly against the F# 10 compiler: a match over a
    six-case DU missing THREE cases reports exactly one of them. So a
    warning that reports one instance of an N-instance problem produces
    an N-instance backlog, and the backlog is invisible because the build
    is green. That is not theoretical here — one hygiene bundle found a
    label function missing two arms where the warning had recorded one,
    and an audit-event table that had fallen 51 of 132 cases behind.

    Promoting it to an error does not make the message list every missing
    case — it still names one — but it forces the fix loop to CONTINUE
    until the match is exhaustive, instead of letting it stop after the
    single arm the warning happened to name.

    SCOPE. Tree-wide: every project that inherits this file. That is all
    161 projects in ToolUp.Forge.sln, plus Build.fsproj, tools/*,
    probes/*, dev/*, samples/*, and the templates/ projects that have no
    Directory.Build.props of their own. A subtree whose own
    Directory.Build.props does not import a parent stops MSBuild's walk
    before it reaches this file, so the rule holds there only if that
    props restates it (listed below) or the subtree is a named exemption.
    The fitness gate DISCOVERS every such props (Phase 1044) and fails,
    naming the file, on one that is neither.

    NOT COVERED, deliberately, and named here so the policy is not read
    as stronger than it is:
      • docs-snippets/ — documentation snippets are prose that must
        RESOLVE, not production code that must be lint-clean. It sits
        outside the solution, its sources are generated + gitignored, and
        it already turns all warnings off (Phase 620).
      • templates/safer/ and templates/platformsdk-solution/ — template
        CONTENT shipped to consumers via `dotnet new`. Each carries its
        own Directory.Build.props that does not import a parent, so
        MSBuild's walk stops there and this file never applies. Neither
        has a scaffold gate that instantiates and builds it, so a rule
        stated there would be asserted on code nothing in this repository
        compiles; they stay exempt until such a gate exists.
    All three exemptions are mirrored in `ArchitectureFitness.fs0025ExemptPrefixes`.

    templates/platformsdk-module-packaged/ and templates/platformsdk-dataplatform/
    are NOT exempt (Phase 1044). Their own Directory.Build.props stop this
    file's walk too, but each states the FS0025 promotion itself, so an
    instantiated module or data platform is born holding exhaustiveness as
    an error. Their scaffold gates (VerifyPackagedModuleTemplate,
    VerifyDataPlatformTemplate in Build.fs) build the instantiated copy, so
    an incomplete match in either template fails its gate by name.

    proofs/oracle/ — the F* extractor's output (Phase 787) — is NOT an
    exemption, though its own Directory.Build.props stops this file's walk
    for the reasons it records. That file states the FS0025 promotion
    itself (Phase 1037), so the generated oracle is under the rule exactly
    as authored code is: the normaliser completes the extractor's
    incomplete matches with explicit failing arms (Phase 1035), the proof
    leg drops the FS0025 id from the `#nowarn` line Custard's backend
    writes, and an extraction shape neither handles fails the build by
    name rather than compiling into a silent warning.

    THE WILDCARD HAZARD. Making this an error creates pressure to write
    `| _ ->` to get green. Do not. Measured: a wildcard emits no
    diagnostic at all, so it silences the check permanently and lets the
    table drift again invisibly — which is exactly how the 51-case gap
    opened. Enumerate the missing arms instead; if a case is genuinely
    unreachable, match it explicitly and fail loudly in its body.

    The two BROADER escape hatches — `#nowarn "25"` (whole file) and
    FS0025 in a project's NoWarn / WarningsNotAsErrors (whole project) —
    are pinned at zero by the Phase 624 tests in
    src/ToolUp.Platform.Tests/InProcess/ArchitectureFitnessTests.fs,
    which also assert this property is still here.

    Where a table over a closed DU must PROVABLY stay in step, the
    wildcard-immune mechanism is a reflection census over
    FSharpType.GetUnionCases — see AuditEventRegistryTests (Phase 114).
    It never reads the match expression, so no wildcard can fool it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);FS0025</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Phase 261 (2026-09-12) — XML documentation files are EMITTED, repo-wide.

    WHY. The 1.0 surface should be the DOCUMENTED surface: an adopter meets
    the SDK through hover-docs and the generated reference, not the source.
    Two things follow from emitting the file, and only the first is the
    obvious one:

      * the `.xml` sidecar is packed beside each `lib/net10.0/*.dll`, so a
        consumer's IDE has tooltips at all. Without it, every `///` this
        repo already carries is invisible the moment the package crosses a
        project boundary — the docs exist and reach nobody.
      * it is the only machine-readable statement of WHICH members carry a
        doc comment, which is what the Phase 261 coverage gate measures.
        See `docs/platform/doc-coverage.md`.

    SCOPE — repo-wide, and deliberately not conditioned on `IsPackable`.
    `Directory.Build.props` is imported BEFORE the project body, so
    `$(IsPackable)` is not yet set here and a condition on it would read as
    empty for every project. The three projects that genuinely must not
    emit one already say so in their own `.fsproj` — which is evaluated
    after this file and therefore wins:
      • docs-snippets/ToolUp.DocSnippets.fsproj
      • src/Hosts/Docker/ToolUp.Hosts.Docker.fsproj
      • templates/safer-pack/ToolUp.Templates.SAFER.fsproj
    Everything else emitting a sidecar is harmless: a test project's
    unused `.xml` costs a few hundred KB of `obj/` and is not packed
    (`IsPackable=false` ships no lib/).

    WARNINGS. F# has NO equivalent of C#'s CS1591 ("missing XML comment for
    publicly visible type or member") — the F# compiler does not warn on an
    undocumented member, so turning this on adds no missing-doc noise and
    the coverage question stays with the gate, where it can be scoped to
    the tracked public surface rather than to every internal binding. What
    IT DOES turn on is FS3390 (malformed XML in a doc comment), which is a
    real defect in a real doc comment and is left at warning severity: it
    is not in `WarningsAsErrors` above, so it cannot break a build, and it
    names the file and line for whoever fixes it.

    Measured on the untouched tree before landing: see the census in
    `docs/platform/doc-coverage.md`.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>Andrew J. Willshire</Authors>
    <Company>ToolUp Analytics Ltd (UK)</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) Andrew J. Willshire / ToolUp Analytics Ltd (UK)</Copyright>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/ToolUp-Forge/toolup-forge</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/ToolUp-Forge/toolup-forge</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageTags>fsharp;full-stack;giraffe;fable;elmish;feliz</PackageTags>
    <!--
      0.21.0 (2026-08-26) — MINOR, not a patch. `GoogleCloudStorageConfig`
      and `AwsSecretsManagerConfig` each gained an `EndpointUrl` field, and
      an added field on an F# record breaks every construction expression
      for it, so a patch here would swap the contract under consumers
      pinned to 0.20.1 in a same-version repack. That is the exact defect
      Phases 647-651 left behind on 0.18.0; do not repeat it.
    -->
    <!--
      0.23.0 (2026-08-28) — MINOR. Phase 739 added a case to the
      `AuditEvent` union (`MediaKeyDelivered`). A DU case addition breaks
      every exhaustive `match` over the union in consumer code, and the
      record-widening dispensation does not cover it — worse, against a
      same-version repack it fails at RUN time as an `InvalidCastException`
      rather than at compile time, because the consumer's warm NuGet cache
      keeps serving the narrower union. So the slot must advance, not be
      rewritten.

      The same slot also carries Phase 741, which added two abstract
      members to `IBlobStorage` (`CanComposeFrom` / `ComposeFrom`). That
      is breaking for IMPLEMENTORS in the same class as Phase 455's
      `DownloadRange` — every custom store stops compiling until it
      answers them — and MINOR is where this repo's SemVer-on-0.x policy
      puts a break. It is deliberately NOT a second bump: 739's raise
      already carries the slot past `v0.22.0`, and a second one here
      would publish a version nobody asked for to say the same thing.

      The same slot ALSO carries Phase 743, on the same reasoning as 741.
      It adds an abstract member to `IDeliveredLogSource`
      (`ByteSemantics`), which is breaking for implementors in exactly
      741's class, and widens three records (`DeliveredBatch`,
      `DeliveredIngestOutcome`, `PlaybackRollup`) plus `FieldMap` under
      the operator's 2026-08-04 record-widening dispensation. Deliberately
      not a third bump: the newest tag is still `v0.22.0`, so 0.23.0 has
      never been published and 743's surface will ship in the same
      artefact as 739's and 741's. A version is bumped when the slot has
      been RELEASED, not once per phase that touches it.

      The same slot ALSO carries Phase 737, on that last sentence. It
      widens three `ToolUp.PublicRendering` records —
      `SitemapGenerator.SitemapHandlerOptions` (a `Universe` field),
      `StaticExportOptions` (`SitemapUniverse`) and
      `PublicRenderingCompose.PublicRenderingServerApp`
      (`SitemapExcludeNoindex`) — under the same 2026-08-04 dispensation,
      breaking only a consumer constructing one as a full record literal.
      Everything else it adds is additive, and the new field defaults
      reproduce the pre-737 sitemap byte for byte. `v0.22.0` is still the
      newest tag, so 0.23.0 remains unpublished and 737 ships in that same
      artefact rather than asking for a fourth number.

      And the same slot carries Phase 752, riding it for the same reason.
      It adds one function to `ToolUp.Graph.Neo4j`'s `CypherTranslation`
      (`isMalformedStatementCode`) — purely additive — and changes what
      `classifyError` RETURNS for two inputs it was getting wrong: a
      driver exception wrapped by `Async.AwaitTask` in an
      `AggregateException` (previously `StorageFailure` for everything,
      so the whole retryable/non-retryable split GP 12 rule 3 requires
      was dead), and a `Neo.ClientError.Statement.ParameterMissing`
      (previously `StorageFailure`, now `MalformedQuery`). Both are
      corrections toward the interface's stated contract rather than a
      contract change, and the newest tag is STILL `v0.22.0`, so 0.23.0
      remains unpublished and this ships in the same artefact as the
      phases above. Same rule, applied again: released, not per-phase.

      And the same slot ALSO carries Phase 749, which is the largest break
      in it: it DELETES two packages, `ToolUp.AuthProviders.EntraExternalId`
      and `ToolUp.AuthProviders.EntraExternalId.Client`, executing the
      removal their 0.4.0 soft deprecation scheduled as `0.Y.0`. It also
      removes seven now-unreachable public literals from
      `ToolUp.Platform.Core` (the six `ConfigKeys.Names.entraExternalId*`
      env-var names and `AuthMetrics.EntraClaimParseFailed`), which the
      deleted companion was the only reader and only emitter of.

      MINOR is where this repo's SemVer-on-0.x policy puts a break, and
      the no-further-bump reasoning is 743's and 737's verbatim: the newest
      tag is still `v0.22.0`, so 0.23.0 has never been published and this
      removal ships in the same artefact as the others. A consumer pinned
      to 0.22.0 is untouched and keeps both packages; the migration recipe
      for the one that upgrades is
      `docs/migrations/0.23.0-entra-external-id-removal.md`.

      A package that STOPS being produced is worth stating explicitly,
      because it is the one shape a version number cannot express: there
      is no 0.23.0 of either companion to resolve, so a consumer that
      raises its pin without dropping the `PackageReference` fails at
      RESTORE (`NU1101`) rather than at compile. That is the intended
      failure — loud, early, and naming the package — and it is why the
      removal is a minor rather than something quieter.

      The same slot also carries Phase 797, which added a `ResolvedScope`
      overload of every scoped member to `IFactStore` and to
      `IFactDisclosureGate`, and retyped the three fact tools' `executeWith`
      executors from `string` to `ResolvedScope`. Breaking for IMPLEMENTORS
      of either interface in the same class as 741's `IBlobStorage` members
      (a custom store or gate stops compiling until it answers them; the
      string members are untouched), and MINOR is where the SemVer-on-0.x
      policy puts a break. Not a second bump, for 741's reason: the slot is
      already past `v0.22.0` and unpublished. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/797-scope-choke-point.md`.
    -->
    <!--
      0.25.1 - the release of the 0.25.0 draft below, unchanged in content.
      The 0.25.0 slot was packed as a draft more than once while Phases 1001
      and 1002 moved its public surface, so the number 0.25.0 names more than
      one contract and is never released: a released version names one. Read
      the 0.25.0 entries as the 0.25.1 contract; a consumer moving from v0.24.1
      reads every migration they cite.
    -->
    <!--
      0.25.0 (draft, never released - see 0.25.1) - MINOR. Phase 995 adds a case to the
      `GroundedNarrativeOutcome` union (`GroundedNarrativeUnfunded`): a
      grounded narrative run whose scope holds no AI provider the funding
      policy allows is now a typed, permanent outcome rather than a
      `GroundedNarrativeFailed` the data-arrival trigger retried. A DU case
      addition breaks every exhaustive `match` over the union in consumer
      code, which is Phase 739's class, so the slot advances: `v0.24.1` is
      tagged and released, and a released version names one contract.

      The same phase adds `AccessContext.forConfigScope` to
      `ToolUp.Platform.Core` (additive), and changes what a run's
      `GroundedNarrativeRequest.Access = None` resolves the provider under:
      the scope the run carries rather than the definition's principal, so
      a team's own key funds that team's reports. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/995-grounded-run-provider-scope.md`.

      Phase 994 rides the same draft slot. `IFactTableWriter.OpenRun` gains
      an optional `FactTableRunOptions` argument (the inputs a run was
      computed from, and the period slice it replaces), and
      `FactTableWriteError` gains two cases (`FactTableRunInProgress`: a
      table takes one open run per scope; `FactTableRunOptionsRefused`).
      Breaking for IMPLEMENTORS and decorators of the writer, and for an
      exhaustive `match` over the error union; additive for callers.
      `InputHash` (`ToolUp.Facts.Core`) and `FactTableRunOptions`
      (`ToolUp.Facts.Server`) are new. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/994-fact-run-inputs-and-periods.md`.

      Phase 996 rides the same draft slot. A published page can serve a
      publication audience: `PageAudience.Publication` (readers named by
      directory role, and the publishing scope the page reads),
      `ClaimMapping.PublicationReaders` (admits a principal the member rule
      refuses as a publication reader, confined to its publications), and
      `PublicationRead` / `PublicationAudit` (the publishing-scope read,
      disclosure re-check and audit). Breaking for full record literals of
      `AuthenticatedUser`, `AccessContext` and `ClaimMapping` (each gains a
      field: `Admission`, `Admission`, `PublicationReaders`) and for an
      exhaustive `match` over `PageAudience`; behaviour is unchanged until
      `PublicationReaders` is set. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/996-publication-audience.md`.

      Phase 1001 rides the same draft slot. An app supplies ServerApp its
      secret store: `ServerApp.withSecretStore` (and the `ServerApp.SecretStore`
      field), `composeSecretStore` and `SecretStore.declaresEncryptionAtRest`
      in `ToolUp.Platform.Server`, and an injected-`SecretClient` constructor
      plus `createWithClient` in `ToolUp.Secrets.AzureKeyVault` (additive).
      Breaking for a full `ServerApp` record literal (the record gains a
      field) and for a direct caller of the low-level `compose` (a trailing
      `secretStore` argument); both are the class this slot already carries.
      `encrypted-secret-store-mode` and `oauth-secret-encryption-mode` now
      also pass on a composed store that DECLARES encryption at rest; the
      default and environment-switch paths are unchanged. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/1001-serverapp-secret-store.md`.

      Phase 1002 rides the same draft slot. BREAKING. A publication reader
      is its own subject, resolved ahead of the deployment's Surfaces, so a
      `Publication` page admits its readers whatever subject shapes the app
      serves, and the reader holds no scope of its own. Closed unions gain a
      case: `Subject.PublicationReader`, `SubjectKind.PublicationReaderKind`,
      `AuditSubject.ReaderAudit`, `AuditSubjectKind.ReaderAuditKind` and
      `AuditEvent.PublicationReaderSignedIn` (with its payload record), which
      breaks every exhaustive `match` over them in consumer code - the class
      Phase 995 already advanced this slot for. Additive:
      `SubjectResolution.publicationReader` and
      `StorageScopeDerivation.tryFromSubject`. Behaviour:
      `StorageScopeDerivation.fromSubject` raises for a reader (it holds no
      scope); `AccessContext.canAccessModule` / `hasPermission` are false for
      a reader; and surface enforcement now refuses a reader
      `403 publication_reader_not_admitted` on EVERY `/api` route, including
      routes open to anonymous callers (the anonymous-only `/api/` bridge
      and the query-param SSE routes), which Phase 996 let it through - a
      narrowing. A reader's sign-in is audited as
      `PublicationReaderSignedIn` under `_platform`, never `UserLoggedIn`.
      Unchanged for every deployment that sets no `PublicationReaders`.
      Migration recipe: `docs/migrations/1002-publication-reader-subject.md`.
    -->
    <!--
      0.26.0 (draft) - MINOR. Phase 1000 changes a default: an unconfigured
      forge server now listens on loopback 127.0.0.1 instead of every
      interface, because the host builder no longer hard-codes
      `UseUrls("http://0.0.0.0:<port>")`. All interfaces is an explicit
      deployment choice: `SERVER_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0` or the new
      `ServerConfig.BindAddress = Some "0.0.0.0"`. A changed default is a
      behaviour change, so the slot advances past the released `v0.25.1`
      (there is no draft slot to ride), and MINOR is where the SemVer-on-0.x
      policy puts it.

      Precedence, first match wins: `Kestrel:Endpoints:*` configuration, then
      the `urls` host setting (`ASPNETCORE_URLS`, `DOTNET_URLS`, an `urls`
      key, a launch profile's `applicationUrl`), then `SERVER_BIND_ADDRESS`,
      then `ServerConfig.BindAddress`, then 127.0.0.1. The first two were
      silently overridden by the hard-coded `UseUrls` and now take effect as
      ASP.NET Core documents them, which is a second behaviour change: an
      `ASPNETCORE_URLS` or `applicationUrl` that disagreed with
      `SERVER_PORT` used to lose and now wins. The shipped Dockerfile
      templates move from `ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://+:5000` to
      `SERVER_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0`, so `SERVER_PORT` keeps choosing the
      port inside the image exactly as it did.

      A new preflight, `server-bind-address`, reads the deployed posture
      from three signals (`DOTNET_RUNNING_IN_CONTAINER=true`, which the .NET
      container images set; `KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST`, present in every
      Kubernetes pod; `ServerConfig.ReplicaCount > 1`). A deployed server
      whose every listen URL is loopback is refused (Error) when the
      loopback is the DEFAULT - the change landed under a deployment that
      never chose it, and a server nothing can reach must not start looking
      healthy - and warned (Warning) when loopback was configured
      EXPLICITLY, because a sidecar proxy sharing the pod's network
      namespace reaching the server over loopback is a legitimate topology
      and the operator stated it. Both messages name the signal and the
      setting that changes it. Local runs, all-interfaces deployments and
      `WorkerOnly` processes are Ok.

      Surface: `ServerConfig.BindAddress` widens the record's constructor
      (Core), and `ComposeBootstrap.buildHostBuilder` takes the bind address
      and returns the listen plan beside the two builders - a retype of the
      low-level compose helper. Additive: the `ServerBinding` module and
      `ComposeBootstrap.listenPlanFor` / `applyListenPlan`.
      Migration recipe: `docs/migrations/1000-server-bind-address.md`.
    -->
    <!--
      0.26.0 (draft), Phase 1005 rides it - MINOR class, unchanged. A
      deployment declares a standing report subscription in its composition
      and names the principal it runs as, never a scope. At startup the
      deployment's own scope resolver resolves the principal (the new
      `DeclaredPrincipalResolver`, constructor internal, registered by the
      platform's scope-resolution composition) and the job is scheduled under
      that resolution. Every refusal (undeclared or unresolvable principal, a
      scope that does not persist, a missing template, an invalid
      subscription) fails the start. Seeding is idempotent and a removed
      declaration's row is retired through a ledger.

      Additive: `StandingReportSubscription`, `StandingSubscriptionPrincipal`
      (Reporting.Core); `ReportingCompose.withStandingSubscriptions` /
      `seedStandingSubscriptions` and their records, `IStandingSubscriptionLedger`,
      `ReportSubscriptionApiHandler.seedStanding` (Reporting.Server);
      `DeclaredPrincipalResolver` (Platform.Server). Breaking: the record
      `SubscriptionRunAudit` gains `RunAs`. Behaviour: saving a subscription
      with a new cadence now replaces its job (old cron cancelled); it used to
      keep firing on the old cron.
      Migration recipe: `docs/migrations/1005-standing-report-subscriptions.md`.
    -->
    <!--
      0.27.0 (draft) - Phase 1007 advances the slot (driver cut via the
      version ledger, 2026-10-13): v0.26.0 is tagged at f8150db7, and the
      HNSW companion's `HnswParameters` record gains `ConstructionPruning`
      and `TombstoneRebuildFraction` - a record widening, breaking for
