    <!--
      0.28.0 (draft) - advances MINOR over the untagged 0.27.1 (which carried
      only the MessagePack raise above, and rides along). Phase 1006: a
      publication reader's `/api` refusal was already audited - every refusal
      writes a `SurfaceDenied` row under `_platform` naming the reader, and
      the default `IAuthAuditHook` writes a coalesced `AuthorizationDenied`
      row - and that row now also names the route CLASS it was refused on
      (`ReaderRouteClass`: sse, upload, assistant, team-management,
      anonymous-open, module, api) and the roles it was admitted with.
      `SurfaceDeniedPayload` gains `RouteClass: string option` and
      `ReaderRoles: string list option` (Platform.Core), `None` for every
      other subject and omitted from the row then, so a non-reader row is
      byte-identical to its pre-1006 form and a pre-1006 row decodes. The
      record gaining fields breaks a full-literal construction of
      `SurfaceDeniedPayload` (FS0764: its constructor is retyped), which is
      the class that advances this slot. What does NOT break: the wire (a
      non-reader row is byte-identical), decoding a pre-1006 row (both fields
      read as `None`), and the `AuthorizationDenied` stream (unchanged). Additive (Platform.Server):
      `SurfaceEnforcement.ReaderRouteClass` (the class literals and
      `classify`) and `SurfaceEnforcement.isPublicationReader`. Behaviour: a
      failed audit write on the denial path (thrown, or failing after it
      started) is now logged as a warning and the refusal stands; it was
      swallowed silently. The rate limit is the existing one: the default
      `IAuthAuditHook` coalesces a reader's refusals per route over a
      60-second window into one leading row and one counted summary row
      (`DedupCount`). That window stays: it bounds a probing burst, not the
      rollup horizon, and the per-refusal `SurfaceDenied` row stays unbounded
      because each refusal is a fact the trail must hold. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/1006-reader-refusal-audit.md`.

      Rides this draft, additive (Platform.Server): Phase 1025 adds a
      `WebhookDispatcherService.StopAsync` override, which cancels in-flight
      subscription fan-outs and waits for them at shutdown. Behaviour, no
      surface change: a store write the conversation and result stores, or the
      webhook fan-out, start fire-and-forget can no longer terminate the host.
      A failed write is logged (Warning; the fan-out at Error) and the
      caller's result stands. A lineage store's `Error` result, previously
      dropped silently, now warns too.

      Rides this draft, in the class it already carries (a record gaining a
      field): Phase 1027 adds durable lineage, off by default.
      `ServerConfig.LineageDurability: LineageDurabilityMode` (Platform.Core,
      env `TOOLUP_LINEAGE_DURABILITY`) retypes the `ServerConfig` constructor
      (FS0764 for a full literal; `ServerConfig.defaults with` is unaffected).
      Additive (Platform.Server): `ServerApp.withDurableLineage`,
      `ResultStore.LineageOutbox` / `LineageIntent` / `LineageDeadLetter` /
      `LineageOutboxDefaults`, and `EntityOutboxRelayService.OutboxRelayService`,
      the generic drain both outbox relays now run on. Additive (AI.Server,
      Forms.Server): the `withDurableLineage` twins. Off is byte-identical to
      Phase 1025. On, a result's lineage links are staged and retried,
      deduplicated by link id, until recorded or dead-lettered. Migration
      recipe: `docs/migrations/1027-durable-lineage.md`.

      Rides this draft, additive (Platform.Core): Phase 1016 adds
      `ConfigKeys.Names.publishPushTimeoutSeconds` / `publishPushRetries`
      and their descriptors (`TOOLUP_PUBLISH_PUSH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, default
      900; `TOOLUP_PUBLISH_PUSH_RETRIES`, default 3). Behaviour (Platform.Build,
      no surface change - the push loop is internal): every `Publish` push
      passes a push timeout, a failed package push is retried with backoff, and a
      persistent failure names every package left unpushed.

      Rides this draft, in the class it already carries (a record gaining a
      field): Phase 770 gives seam authority a runtime edge.
      `ComponentSeamAuthority.ObservedReach: SeamId list option`
      (Platform.Server) retypes that record's constructor (FS0764 for a full
      literal); `None` reads as before. Additive (Platform.Server):
      `SeamConfinement`, `SeamAwareServiceProvider` (the type and its
      `confineHandler(s)` / `confineTool(s)` module), `SeamReachObserver`,
      `SeamAuthorityEnforcement.confine` / `confineAudited`,
      `DeploymentVerificationEvidence.withObservedSeamReach`,
      `DeploymentVerificationReport.SeamAuthorityObservedBeyondDerived` and
      `ModuleSurface.substrateSeams`. Behaviour: `ServerApp.addModule` now
      runs each module's route handlers and AI tools through the seam-aware
      provider, which is a passthrough until a composition calls `confine`;
      a deployment that does not is byte-identical. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/770-seam-aware-service-provider.md`.

      Rides this draft, additive (Remoting.Analyzers, an analyser-only package
      with no lib surface): Phase 776 adds the containment rules TUA001 to
      TUA003 (container resolution outside service registration, an HttpClient
      built without the egress handler, Async.RunSynchronously outside an
      entry point) as a second analyser, `ToolUpContainment`, beside the
      unchanged TUR0001 / TUR0002 pair; the `Rules`, `ContainmentRules` and
      `TreeScan` modules; and the generated rule reference
      `docs/platform/analyzer-rules.md`. The package keeps its published id.
      Migration recipe: `docs/migrations/776-toolup-analyzers-rule-set.md`.

      Rides this draft, in the class it already carries (a source-breaking
      addition): Phase 771 observes the hardening baseline at boot.
      `CompositionProfileRefusal` (Platform.Server) gains the case
      `HostingBaselineBelowFloor`, which breaks an exhaustive match on that
      union the way a record gaining a field breaks a full literal; `describe`
      handles it. Additive (Platform.Core): the `HostingBaselineSection`
      literal. Additive (Platform.Server): `HostingBaseline` (`HostingControl`,
      `BaselineControl`, `HostingBaselinePolicy`, `ProbeResponse`,
      `HostingObservation`, `UnknownKeyGuardObservation`), the report's
      `HostingControlState` / `HostingBaselineVerdict` / `HostingControlRecord`
      / `HostingBaselineIntegrity` / `IHostingBaselineEvidence` /
      `HostingBaselineRecord`, `DeploymentVerificationEvidence.withHostingBaseline`
      / `hostingBaselineOf`, and `ServerApp.withHostingBaselinePolicy`.
      Behaviour: `ServerApp.withDeploymentVerification` now has `run` send two
      probe requests through the composed pipeline before it serves, and the
      report gains a thirteenth section; a deployment that mounts no route and
      declares no floor sends nothing and is byte-identical. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/771-hosting-hardening-baseline.md`.

      Rides this draft, in the class it already carries (a record gaining a
      field): Phase 773 holds a declared route to its effects.
      `ServerModule.RouteEffects` (Platform.Server) retypes that record's
      constructor (FS0764 for a full literal); `ServerModule.create` sets it
      to `[]`. Additive (Platform.Server): `ServerModule.withRouteEffect`,
      `RouteEffectDeclaration` and its module, `RouteEffectObserver`,
      `RouteEffectScope`, `RouteEffectContext`, `RouteEffectContainment`
      and `EgressRouteContext`. Additive (Platform.Core):
      `HostCapabilityBoundary` and `HostCapabilityRegistry.installBoundary`.
      Behaviour: a module that declares no route effect is byte-identical; a
      declared route's host-capability invocations go through the Phase 300
      `guardInvoke` with its envelope, and its outbound calls through
      platform clients are held to its egress grant. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/773-declared-route-effect-containment.md`.

      Rides this draft, in the class it already carries (a record gaining a
      field, a union gaining a case): Phase 774 puts a verification level on
      the boot seal. `DeployRecord` (Platform.Core) gains `VerificationLevel`,
      inside the signed bytes under `SchemaVersion` 2; a schema-1 record still
      canonicalises to the bytes it was sealed over and reads as `Attributed`,
      inferred. `BootVerificationOptions` gains `MinimumLevel` and `Attestor`,
      `BootVerificationResult` gains `Level`, `BootVerificationVerdict` gains
      the case `BelowVerificationFloor`, and the report's `BootSealVerified` /
      `BootSealVerifiedUnrecorded` gain a `level` element (Platform.Server):
      each breaks a full literal (FS0764), an exhaustive match or a positional
      pattern. Additive (Platform.Core): `VerificationLevel`,
      `RuntimeObservation`, `IRuntimeAttestor`, `RuntimeAttestor.unattested`,
      `VerificationLevelProvenance`, `VerificationLevelReading` and their
      modules, and `DeployRecord.withVerificationLevel` / `verificationLevel` /
      `effectiveSchemaVersion` / `recordsVerificationLevel`. Behaviour: the
      bytes of a NEWLY created record change once (schema 2); nothing sealed
      before moves, and no floor is declared by default. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/774-verification-level.md`.

      Rides this draft, additive: Phase 1015 lets a deployment declare how a
      data source's data arrives - a scheduled pull or a watched location -
      run as a declared principal, never a scope. Additive (Platform.Server):
      `DataSourceDelivery`, `DeclaredDataSource`, `DeclaredDataSources`,
      `IDeclaredDeliveryLedger`, `DataSourceConfigStore.declaredDeliveryLedger`
      and `BlobDeclaredDeliveryLedger`, and the shared `DeclaredPrincipal`
      record with its `user` / `userOf` / `missingGrant` readings. Additive
      (DataSources.Common): `DeclaredDelivery` (`seed`, `watchHandler`,
      `depsOf`, `startupService`, `withDeclaredDataSources`),
      `DeclaredDeliveryDeps`, `DeclaredDeliveryServices`,
      `DeclaredSeedReport`, `DeclaredDeliveryRefusal`,
      `DeclaredDataSourcesRefused`, `WatchedObject`, `Files.listObjects` and
      `IWatchableFileSource`, which the Csv, Excel and Parquet connectors now
      implement. `DataSourceConfig` is unchanged; a deployment that declares
      no source is byte-identical. Migration recipe:
      `docs/migrations/1015-declared-data-delivery.md`.

      Rides this draft, additive (Platform.Core, Platform.Server): Phase 777
      puts the platform's trust boundaries on every verification report.
      Core gains `TrustBoundaryId`, `TrustClaim`, `ContainmentProfile`,
      `TrustMechanism`, the `TrustBoundary` record, the `TrustBoundaries`
      module (the closed list and the generated public page) and the
      `TrustBoundariesSection` literal. Server gains the route-effect
      evidence member (`DeclaredRouteRecord`, `RouteEffectIntegrity`,
      `IRouteEffectEvidence`, `DeclaredRouteEffects`,
      `DeploymentVerificationEvidence.routeEffectsOf` / `withRouteEffects`)
      and `DeploymentVerificationReport.gatherTrustBoundaries`. No member is
      removed or retyped. Behaviour: every report grows a fourteenth section,
      appended, so every verdict digest moves once; a bare deployment still
      reads nothing-composed. Public page: `docs/platform/trust-boundaries.md`.

      Rides this draft, in the BREAKING class it already carries: Phase 1029
      gives a declared principal one shape across the platform and reporting
      tiers. The `DeclaredPrincipal` record (never released) moves from
      Platform.Server to Platform.Core, same namespace and fields; its
      `user` / `userOf` / `missingGrant` readings stay in Platform.Server.
      `StandingSubscriptionPrincipal` (Reporting.Core) becomes an
      `[<Obsolete>]` abbreviation of `DeclaredPrincipal`: F# source naming it
      still compiles, with a warning, but its own IL class is gone (a binary
      break for an assembly compiled against 0.27.0). Retyped
      (Reporting.Server): `ReportingCompose.StandingSubscriptions.Principals`
      is a `DeclaredPrincipal list`. Deprecated (Reporting.Server):
      `StandingSubscriptionRefusal.missingGrant`, in favour of
      `DeclaredPrincipal.missingGrant`. Behaviour is unchanged. Migration
      recipe: `docs/migrations/1029-one-declared-principal-shape.md`.
    -->
